WEBVTT

00:00.080 --> 00:04.160
Could Exbabel’s flagship AI translation model beat a professional UN

00:04.440 --> 00:06.520
interpreter in a blind comparison?

00:06.560 --> 00:09.040
On the speeches in this study, it did.

00:09.120 --> 00:13.040
Exbabel uses its flagship model for its top twenty languages.

00:13.120 --> 00:16.120
Its other model covers seventy additional languages.

00:16.160 --> 00:18.960
That model scored almost exactly the same as the UN

00:19.160 --> 00:20.120
interpreters.

00:20.160 --> 00:21.880
Here’s how the comparison worked.

00:21.920 --> 00:25.200
These 180 clips came directly from recordings of the General

00:25.280 --> 00:28.613
Debate at the eightieth session of the UN General Assembly.

00:28.667 --> 00:31.680
They captured delegates from fourteen countries speaking in the assembly

00:31.760 --> 00:32.533
hall.

00:32.587 --> 00:35.840
For 55 clips, researchers matched the delegate’s words to the

00:35.920 --> 00:38.520
corresponding passage from the UN interpreter.

00:38.560 --> 00:41.680
Two AI judges read the same passage three ways, with

00:41.720 --> 00:43.000
the names removed.

00:43.040 --> 00:45.960
They compared each translation with the original speech and scored

00:46.000 --> 00:47.240
it out of a hundred.

00:47.280 --> 00:48.600
The question was simple.

00:48.640 --> 00:51.120
If you heard this translation, would you come away with

00:51.160 --> 00:52.503
the delegate’s meaning?

00:52.640 --> 00:57.360
Across the 55 matched passages, Exbabel’s flagship model averaged 91.3

00:57.440 --> 01:00.747
out of 100 for preserving the speaker’s meaning.

01:00.773 --> 01:03.880
The UN interpreters averaged 87.3.

01:03.920 --> 01:09.000
Exbabel’s other model, which serves the remaining languages, averaged 87.1.

01:09.040 --> 01:12.000
That’s four points above the interpreters for the flagship, and

01:12.080 --> 01:14.240
just two tenths of a point below for the other

01:14.600 --> 01:15.000
model.

01:15.040 --> 01:17.400
These scores describe the passages tested.

01:17.440 --> 01:20.987
A 91 doesn’t mean 91 percent of the words matched.

01:21.013 --> 01:23.320
The result also changed with the language.

01:23.360 --> 01:28.000
On the Spanish-to-English clips, Exbabel’s flagship model averaged 99 points

01:28.040 --> 01:30.280
and the interpreters averaged 92.

01:30.320 --> 01:34.120
On the Russian-to-English clips, the interpreters had a slight edge.

01:34.240 --> 01:36.760
So what made this a blind test?

01:36.800 --> 01:39.400
Three fresh AI reviewers shared the work.

01:39.440 --> 01:42.240
Two independently rated each translation.

01:42.320 --> 01:44.840
Each saw different labels and a different order.

01:44.880 --> 01:47.680
The reviewers had no access to the model identities, the

01:47.760 --> 01:51.200
answer key, previous reports, or each other’s scores.

01:51.280 --> 01:56.520
Fifty-five passages, three translations each: 165 translations.

01:56.560 --> 02:00.027
Two ratings apiece gave us 330 judgments.

02:00.053 --> 02:02.640
In a Russian-language speech, a delegate looked back at an

02:02.720 --> 02:05.800
agreement between the Soviet Union and the United States.

02:05.840 --> 02:08.560
It helped stabilize their relationship and made the Cold War

02:08.640 --> 02:10.200
a little more manageable.

02:10.240 --> 02:13.360
Both the flagship and the UN interpretation got that history

02:13.440 --> 02:14.000
across.

02:14.080 --> 02:16.800
The difference came in what the delegate wanted people to

02:16.880 --> 02:17.840
remember.

02:17.920 --> 02:21.520
The UN interpreter said the agreement introduced “the principle of

02:21.640 --> 02:23.360
indivisible security.”

02:23.440 --> 02:26.080
That means the security of one country is tied to

02:26.120 --> 02:27.760
the security of others.

02:27.840 --> 02:31.600
The flagship also kept the speaker’s emphasis: putting that principle

02:31.680 --> 02:35.280
into practice was the agreement’s most important legacy.

02:35.360 --> 02:38.000
Both AI reviewers noted that distinction.

02:38.080 --> 02:40.080
The flagship scored 100.

02:40.160 --> 02:43.440
The UN interpretation scored 95.5.

02:43.520 --> 02:46.560
The other Exbabel recording kept the point about the legacy,

02:46.640 --> 02:50.000
but cut off at “indivisible,” before the listener heard what

02:50.080 --> 02:51.280
was indivisible.

02:51.360 --> 02:53.600
It scored 93.5.

02:53.680 --> 02:55.920
Now listen to the argument in Mandarin.

02:55.960 --> 02:59.360
The delegate said that countries advance together and all suffer

02:59.440 --> 03:00.560
when divided.

03:00.680 --> 03:03.200
Even on a difficult road, they need to unite and

03:03.280 --> 03:05.120
cooperate sincerely.

03:05.200 --> 03:08.160
The UN transcript kept the difficult road and the call

03:08.240 --> 03:09.200
for unity.

03:09.240 --> 03:12.240
But it ended at “when all countries unite.”

03:12.320 --> 03:15.840
What disappeared was what those countries had to do together.

03:15.920 --> 03:19.600
The flagship supplied that missing action: “work together in good

03:19.680 --> 03:20.480
faith.”

03:20.560 --> 03:22.800
Both reviewers also caught a flaw.

03:22.920 --> 03:26.720
Its phrase “we perish” is much stronger than saying everyone

03:26.800 --> 03:28.000
suffers losses.

03:28.080 --> 03:32.240
The other Exbabel model kept sincere cooperation too, then added

03:32.293 --> 03:34.840
“we will succeed” and repeated the ending.

03:34.880 --> 03:38.880
The reviewers’ scores were 93.5 for the flagship, 82 for

03:38.960 --> 03:42.880
the other model, and 76.5 for the UN transcript.

03:43.000 --> 03:46.120
Those judgments tell us what survived in the transcripts.

03:46.160 --> 03:49.360
Checking the recordings matters before attributing a missing phrase to

03:49.440 --> 03:50.533
an interpreter.

03:50.587 --> 03:54.080
In this blind pilot, Exbabel’s flagship earned the highest average

03:54.160 --> 03:56.133
for preserving the speaker’s meaning.

03:56.187 --> 03:59.627
In one speech, that meant keeping why an agreement mattered.

03:59.653 --> 04:03.733
In the other, it meant saying what cooperation actually required.

04:03.787 --> 04:06.960
And even the highest-scoring translation could make a speaker sound

04:07.040 --> 04:08.680
more dramatic than they were.

04:08.920 --> 04:12.200
The next test puts bilingual listeners in the audience.

04:12.240 --> 04:15.240
Give them a headset, play the speech once, and ask:

04:15.280 --> 04:16.640
what did the speaker mean?
